92186045499 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166474 1084 #uuid "d5dc64fb-366a-50d0-b0de-982441d7d6c0" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166475 1081 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨ - 1,-1,-1⟩" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166475 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166475 1084 #uuid "dd03ee71-8291-5f27-8e14-186f1aff231e" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166476 1081 "\\frac{1}{2}⟨ - 1, 1, 2⟩" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166476 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166476 1084 #uuid "fd08544a-892b-5d85-961b-7deb4532cf5a" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166477 1081 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨ - 1,-1, 2⟩" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166477 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166477 1084 #uuid "3b18103a-c14d-523c-b469-c099662d1c5f" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166479 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166480 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166481 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166482 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186166483 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166478 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166478 1077 "selection" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166478 1080 #uuid "d7cf5d3f-883e-56cd-b492-c81be00a357b" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166479 1081 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}}⟨ - 2, t, 0⟩" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166479 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166479 1084 #uuid "d21ad4e3-4e8d-5160-8f7e-14708fcd74c1" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166480 1081 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}⟨0, 1, t⟩" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166480 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166480 1084 #uuid "d3761073-5b4f-5b81-8819-8784f4f2c469" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166481 1081 "⟨1, 0, 0⟩" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166481 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166481 1084 #uuid "dc134c18-0277-5cf2-9869-346e1c845030" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166482 1081 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1, 1, 0⟩" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166482 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166482 1084 #uuid "130e2e03-4140-5166-982d-9744a243c698" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166483 1081 "⟨0, 0,-1⟩" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166483 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166483 1084 #uuid "758c15f2-4a2e-5fb1-8073-7582beee784c" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166484 1069 "e-10133" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166484 1071 "Evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^3 \\sqrt{\\sin x + 1} \\right)$ at $x=1.6$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166484 1072 #uuid "107da9c2-2da9-572f-a2bf-bdf35b8c6dcf" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166484 1074 "First, we need to make sure that the calculator is in $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{RAD}\\,}$ (radians) mode.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}$ button. You may need to press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button first.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left.\\left(x^3 \\sqrt{\\sin x + 1} \\right) \\right|_{x=1.6} \\approx 10.818\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166485 1069 "e-10152" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166485 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{x} - x}{x^2 - x \\cos{x}}$.\n\nFind all the discontinuities of $f(x)$ on the interval $x \\in [-3,3]$. Round you answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166485 1072 #uuid "11674946-2411-53c5-8e26-fc58dfcfee39" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166485 1074 "The function $f(x)$ has discontinuities at points where the denominator\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2 - x \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nis equal to zero.\n\nFirst, we plot $y=g(x)$ using a graphing calculator, zooming or adjusting the window ranges to better view the roots.\n\nIn our case, the appropriate window ranges could be the following:\n\n- for the horizontal axis, $x \\in [-3, 3]$\n- for the vertical axis, $y \\in [-2,2]$\n\nThis gives us the following graph:\n\n![](images/4c/4c1a1792cd5b144ed30e469e1ca347d32d02f9f3e8f450705893f15b78d49427.png)\n\nThe graph shows two roots that lie inside the intervals $x \\in [-1,0.5]$ and $x \\in [0.5, 1]$.\n\nUsing our graphing calculator's root-finding function, we find that the roots of $g(x)$ are\n\n$\\qquad$ $x = 0 \\quad$ and\n\n$$\n\\quad x \\approx 0.74\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ has discontinuities ar $x=0$ and\n\n$$\nx\\approx 0.74\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166486 1069 "e-10153" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166486 1071 "$f(x) =\\dfrac{e^{1/(1-2x)}\\sin x}{x \\ln(4x^2 - 4x + 2)}$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has discontinuities at $a=0$ and $b =0.5$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0^+} f(x) = \\infty$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0.5^-} f(x) = \\infty$\n3. $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0.5^+} f(x) = 0$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166486 1072 #uuid "ca5c8593-e684-5a7e-9100-d74ead55b816" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166486 1074 "We can plot the graph of $y=f(x)$ using a graphing calculator, zooming or adjusting the window ranges to get a better view of the local behavior, as shown below.\n\nIn our case, the appropriate window ranges could be the following:\n\n- for the horizontal axis, $x \\in [-1.5, 1.5]$\n- for the vertical axis, $y \\in [0,30]$\n\nThis gives us the following graph:\n\n![](images/be/be3ed8feb62bdf27cb2fc1c066899d026b3bb0a704dc20566ceed36edb043260.png)\n\nLet's now examine the graph.\n\n- Statement I is false. The limit at $x=0$ is finite.\n- Statement II is true. To the left of $x=0.5$, the graph increases to $\\infty$.\n- Statement III is true. To the right of $x=0.5$, the graph approaches $0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166487 1069 "e-10154" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166487 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2 + 1)}{x^2 + x}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is increasing on $x \\in (2,4)$\n2. Rounded to three decimal places, $f'(3) \\approx -0.062$\n3. Rounded to three decimal places, $\\displaystyle\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.393$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166487 1072 #uuid "163e03aa-a3e2-5af6-917b-c3bac65e815e" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166487 1074 "We can plot the graph of $y=f(x)$ using a graphing calculator, zooming or adjusting the window ranges for each statement to get a better view of the local behavior.\n\nIn this case, appropriate window ranges could be the following:\n\n- for the horizontal axis, $x \\in [-1,5]$\n- for the vertical axis, $y \\in [-1,1]$\n\nThis gives the graph below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf59a94ae74182b8e4fa60f50ddcfbf23e5c4041c901789effddf4734d0853be.png)\n\nLet's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph below shows that the function is decreasing on $x \\in (2,4)$.\n\n![](images/44/44344c22138c44ddc45e3ef0fbe0b54e3d47f9e6c5288749b3caa9f3e1a5b2a2.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. To find the derivative, follow these steps:First, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$), then $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$ and select the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,}$ option. Then, we need to enter the point at which to compute the derivative. So, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$, type $X=3$, and then press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{enter}\\,}$. Following this process gives\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -0.061\\,931$.\nRounding the answer to three decimal places, we conclude that $f'(3) \\approx -0.062$.\n- Statement III is true. To find the integral, follow these steps:First, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$), then $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$ and select the $\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\\,}$ option. Then, we select the lower limit. So, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$, type $X=2$, and then press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{enter}\\,}$. Next, we select the upper limit. So, we press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$, type $X=4$, and then press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{enter}\\,}$. Following this process gives\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 0.392\\,602$.\nRounding the answer to three decimal places, we conclude that $\\displaystyle\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\,\\textrm d x \\approx 0.393$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166488 1069 "e-10418" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166488 1071 "A travel agency offers three itineraries, itinerary $1$, itinerary $2$, and $3$. For each itinerary, customers can choose to travel using their own car or the agency's bus.\n\n- Itinerary $1$ is chosen by $25\\%$ of the customers, and $60\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n- Itinerary $2$ is chosen by $30\\%$ of the customers, and $30\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n- Itinerary $3$ is chosen by $45\\%$ of the customers, and $20\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\nIf $65\\%$ of all customers travel with children, and $40\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses the bus travels with children? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166488 1072 #uuid "bc694d40-d150-55e6-981f-0ca6b5d91e21" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166488 1074 "Let $C$ be the event that a customer travels with children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and $I_1, I_2, I_3$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1$, $2$, or $3$, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}P(I_{1}) = 0.25 & P(B \\mid I_{1}) = 0.6 \\\\ P(I_{2}) = 0.3 & P(B \\mid I_{2}) = 0.3 \\\\ P(I_{3}) = 0.45 & P(B \\mid I_{3}) = 0.2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe disjoint union $I_1 \\sqcup I_2 \\sqcup I_3$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n$P(I_1)+ P(I_2) + P(I_3) = 0.25+0.3+0.45 = 1$.\n\n- Since $65\\%$ of customers have children, we have\n$P(C) = 0.65$.\n- Since $40\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n$P(B \\mid C) = 0.4$.\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses the bus travels with children, which is represented as $P(C \\mid B)$.\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(C\\begin{vmatrix}B) &= (P(B \\mid C)P(C))/(P(B)) \\\\ = (P(B \\mid C)P(C))/(P(B \\mid I_{1})P(I_{1}) + P(B \\mid I_{2})P(I_{2}) + P(B\\end{vmatrix}I_{3})P(I_{3})) \\\\\n&= ((0.4)(0.65))/((0.6)(0.25) + (0.3)(0.3) + (0.2)(0.45)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0.26}{0.33} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.788\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166489 1069 "e-10514" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166489 1071 "![](images/0a/0acf0860e5ce913b0962b57ff42fa2d34a130c3255157914ef7f43ec58d082b1.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ (shown above) is defined as\n$f(x) = \\sin\\left(1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\ln x} \\right)$.\n\nFind an approximation for the tangent line $\\mathcal T$ to $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=0.7$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166489 1072 #uuid "b76f4523-d424-5c27-bbcb-86d130c39d1d" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166489 1074 "Evaluating our function at the point $x=0.7$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(0.7\\right)=\\sin\\left(1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\ln(0.7)} \\right) \\approx -0.973\\,0\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=0.7$.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}$ button. You may need to press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button first.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\nf'\\left(0.7\\right) = \\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\sin\\left(1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\ln x} \\right) \\right) \\right|_{x=0.7} \\approx 2.591\\,5\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=2.591\\,5$ and the coordinates of our point\n\n$$\n\\left(0.7, -0.973\\,0\\right)\n$$\n\nand get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-0.9730) &= 2.5915(x - 0.7) \\\\\ny + 0.9730 &= 2.5915x - 1.8141 \\\\\ny &= 2.5915x - 2.7871\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate tangent line is given by\n\n$$\ny = 2.6x - 2.8\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166490 1069 "e-10983" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166490 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$f(x) =\\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+8)}{x^2+1}$.\n\nFind an approximation for the normal line to $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=2$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166490 1072 #uuid "091d00c6-e943-530c-966e-264fbc55f92f" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166490 1074 "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny = f(2) = \\dfrac{\\ln((2)^2+8)}{(2)^2+1} \\approx 0.497\\, 0\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=2$.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}$ button. You may need to press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button first.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\nf'(2) = \\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+8)}{x^2+1} \\right) \\right|_{x=2} \\approx -0.330\\, 9\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm = -\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)} = -\\dfrac{1}{-0.330\\, 9} = 3.022\\, 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=3.022\\, 1$ and the coordinates of our point $(2, 0.497\\, 0)$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 0.4970 &= 3.0221(x - 2) \\\\\ny - 0.4970 &= 3.0221x - 6.0442 \\\\\ny &= 3.0221x - 5.5472\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny = 3.0x - 5.5\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166491 1069 "e-12356" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166491 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}$, where\n\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \\quad \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!, \\quad \\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}$.\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166491 1072 #uuid "ac2a2137-53fa-5121-b541-02e0addd8757" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166491 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}$. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖) \\\\\n&= (⟨z,-y,-x⟩)/(\\sqrt{(z)^{2} + (-y)^{2} + (-x)^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{z^{2} + y^{2} + x^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨x, 2z, z - y⟩ \\cdot \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨x, 2z, z - y⟩ \\cdot ⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}} \\\\\n&= (xz - 2yz - x(z - y))/(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{xy - 2yz}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)\\frac{x y - 2 y z}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}dS\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166492 1069 "e-12384" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166492 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle z,\\: z,\\: 4xy\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166492 1072 #uuid "fba5ed01-4c4c-50f6-8060-76bf40df79b0" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166492 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for\n\n$$\n0\\leq x\\leq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0\\leq y\\leq 1\n$$\n\nthe downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨z, z, 4xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1, 4xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\n&=-4xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)(-4xy)dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-4xy)dA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}(-4xy)dxdy \\\\\n&=-\\int_{0}^{1}2ydy\\int_{0}^{1}2xdx \\\\\n&= - [y^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [x^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166493 1069 "e-12454" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166493 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle y,\\: 0,\\: z\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$.\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf r_x' \\times \\mathbf r_y' \\mid \\mid =3$.*" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166493 1072 #uuid "35c3c9ba-60b0-5bcf-8e9c-2862ece44e7e" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166493 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface\n\n$$\nz = 2x+2y\n$$\n\ncan be written using the dot product as\n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = 0, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 0\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S$, we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖) \\\\\n&= (⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩)/(\\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 1^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nz=2x+2y\n$$\n\non $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨y, 0, z⟩ \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}⟨y, 0, 2x + 2y⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(-2y + 0x + 2x + 2y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(2x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)\\frac{2}{3}xdS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot \\mid \\mid r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′} \\mid \\mid dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot 3dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot 3dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2xdA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2}2xdxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}dy \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}2xdx \\\\\n&= [y]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [x^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166494 1069 "e-12490" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166494 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$f(x,y) =\\dfrac{xy}2, \\qquad D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2y, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}$.\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b)$. Find $a$ given that $b=2$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166494 1072 #uuid "22a44ff4-55e7-51db-a54e-26ef23764fa4" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166494 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∬_(D)\\frac{xy}{2}dA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{0}^{2y}\\frac{xy}{2}dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}[\\frac{x^{2}y}{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = 2y}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}y^{3}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{y^{4}}{4}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4} \\\\\n&= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea(D) &= ∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{0}^{2y}dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}[x]_{x = 0}^{x = 2y}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}2ydy \\\\\n&= [y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2$. So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n64 &= f(a, 2) \\cdot 16 \\\\\n64 &= \\frac{a \\cdot 2}{2} \\cdot 16 \\\\\n64 &= 16a \\\\\na &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\left(4,2 \\right) \\in D\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $a = 4$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166495 1069 "e-12491" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166495 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = xy$ on the region $D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}$ given that the area of the region is $3$ square units." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166495 1072 #uuid "0a06a8ae-69d6-5ded-979e-149f650a1676" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166495 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{ave} &= (1)/(Area(D))∬_(D)f(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∬_(D)xydA \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{x}^{3x}xydydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{-1}^{2}[\\frac{xy^{2}}{2}]_{y = x}^{y = 3x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{-1}^{2}4x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[x^{4}]_{x =-1}^{x = 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(16 - 1) \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166496 1069 "e-12492" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166496 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = 15xy^2$ on the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\geq 0$, and $y \\leq 0$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166496 1072 #uuid "13800660-08b4-50af-93b4-1013bbf41e66" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166496 1074 "First, let's sketch our region $D$:\n\n![](images/a3/a381fede820f9fc5ba08bb9569906d41a3e8f779de38b446c4f271ceef3a5ccd.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac\\pi 4\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\right\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)15xy^{2}dA &= ∬_(Δ)15r\\cos θ \\cdot (r\\sin θ)^{2}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{3π/2}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{1}15r^{4}\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ\\int_{0}^{1}5r^{4}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ[r^{5}]_{0}^{1}dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ(1^{5} - 0)dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdθ \\\\\n&= [\\sin^{3} θ]_{3π/2}^{2π} \\\\\n&= (\\sin^{3} (2π) - \\sin^{3} (\\frac{3π}{2})) \\\\\n&= (0 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi} \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166497 1069 "e-12659" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166497 1071 "Find the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2 - 5x_2^2 +3x_3^2$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166497 1072 #uuid "b8306278-726a-5c09-86cc-14631a6abb66" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166497 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\nx_1^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_2^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n2x_1^2 \\leq 3x_1^2, \\qquad -5x_2^2 \\leq 3x_2^2\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 2x_{1}^{2} - 5x_{2}^{2} + 3x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&\\le 3x_{1}^{2} + 3x_{2}^{2} + 3x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\nx_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 =1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n3(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2}) = 3(1) = 3\n$$\n\nand, in turn,\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 3\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no greater than $3$. But before we conclude that $3$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = 3\n$$\n\nNotice that when $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 2x_{1}^{2} - 5x_{2}^{2} + 3x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2(0)^{2} - 5(0)^{2} + 3(1)^{2} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nthe maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $3$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166498 1069 "e-12660" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166498 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=5x_1^2 - 4x_2^2 - 6x_3^2$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166498 1072 #uuid "5b4117f3-62fb-55c6-8e23-0cadcd4a2ec5" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166498 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\nx_1^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_2^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n5x_1^2 \\geq -6x_1^2, \\qquad -4x_2^2 \\geq -6x_2^2\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 5x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&\\ge - 6x_{1}^{2} - 6x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&=-6(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\nx_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 =1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n-6(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2}) =-6(1) =-6\n$$\n\nand, in turn,\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq -6\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no smaller than $-6$. But before we conclude that $-6$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = -6\n$$\n\nNotice that when $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 5x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 5(0)^{2} - 4(0)^{2} - 6(1)^{2} \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nthe minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $-6$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166499 1069 "e-12661" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166499 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2^2-2x_3^2+2x_2x_3$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166499 1072 #uuid "3cec995b-8918-5c48-927e-b0807a947518" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166499 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that:\n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$, is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n$\\max \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}$\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$, is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n$\\min \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 - λ & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 - λ & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)((-2 - λ)(-2 - λ) - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)(λ^{2} + 4λ + 3) &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)(λ + 3)(λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 1,-1,-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = -3\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166500 1069 "e-15052" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166500 1071 "Consider the binomial random variable $X\\sim B(7, 0.63)$. Rounding to $4$ decimal places, fill in the missing values in the cumulative distribution function table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\leq x)$ | $0.0009$ | $0.0123$ | $0.0701$ | $0.2341$ | $0.5134$ | $0.7987$ |  |  |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166500 1072 #uuid "56d68682-0e4d-57e5-9441-997cf4d33f29" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166500 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function for the Binomial distribution $B(n,p)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=7$ and $p=0.63$.\n\nTherefore, we compute the missing elements of the table as follows:\n\n- For $F(6)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(6) &= P(X \\le 6) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le 5) + P(X = 6) \\\\\n&= F(5) + P(X = 6) \\\\\n&= 0.7987 + (\\frac{7}{6})(0.63)^{6}(1 - 0.63)^{7 - 6} \\\\\n&= 0.7987 + 7 \\cdot (0.63)^{6} \\cdot (0.37)^{1} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.9606\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Since $n=7$, we have that $F(7) = P(X\\leq 7) = 1$.\n\nTherefore, our completed table looks as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\leq x)$ | $0.0009$ | $0.0123$ | $0.0701$ | $0.2341$ | $0.5134$ | $0.7987$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.9606}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166501 1069 "e-15054" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166501 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the binomial random variable $X \\sim B(6, 0.67)$ given below, compute $P(X=2)$.\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.0013\\,$ | $\\,0.0170\\,$ | $\\,0.0969\\,$ | $\\,0.3130\\,$ | $\\,0.6422\\,$ | $\\,0.9095\\,$ | $\\,1.0000\\,$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166501 1072 #uuid "92a73e93-f882-5329-a19c-00a6a270bced" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166501 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(2) &= P(X \\le 2) \\\\\n&= P(X ∈ {0, 1, 2}) \\\\\n&= P(X ∈ {0, 1}) + P(X = 2) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le 1) + P(X = 2) \\\\\n&= F(1) + P(X = 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 2) &= F(2) - F(1) \\\\\n&= 0.0969 - 0.0170 \\\\\n&= 0.0799\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166502 1069 "e-15055" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166502 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the binomial random variable $X \\sim B(6, 0.47)$ given below, compute $P(1 \\leq X \\lt 5)$.\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.0222\\,$ | $\\,0.1401\\,$ | $\\,0.4015\\,$ | $\\,0.7107\\,$ | $\\,0.9163\\,$ | $\\,0.9892\\,$ | $\\,1.0000\\,$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166502 1072 #uuid "c80fb077-2d31-522b-8083-5a72613f84de" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166502 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(4) &= P(X \\le 4) \\\\\n&= P(X ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}) \\\\\n&= P(X ∈ {0}) + P(X ∈ {1, 2, 3, 4}) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le 0) + P(1 \\le X < 5) \\\\\n&= F(0) + P(1 \\le X < 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(1 \\le X < 5) &= F(4) - F(0) \\\\\n&= 0.9163 - 0.0222 \\\\\n&= 0.8941\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166503 1069 "e-15056" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166503 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the binomial random variable $X \\sim B(7, 0.52)$ given below, compute $P(X \\geq 2)$.\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.0059\\,$ | $\\,0.0504\\,$ | $\\,0.1951\\,$ | $\\,0.4563\\,$ | $\\,0.7393\\,$ | $\\,0.9233\\,$ | $\\,0.9897\\,$ | $\\,1.0000\\,$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166503 1072 #uuid "c9a6a708-925d-54aa-ac0a-023e5259d19a" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166503 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X > a) = 1 - P(X\\leq a) = 1-F(a)\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2) = P(X \\gt 1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 2) &= P(X > 1) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(X \\le 1) \\\\\n&= 1 - F(1) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.0504 \\\\\n&= 0.9496\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166504 1069 "e-15057" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166504 1071 "Consider the Poisson distribution $\\textrm{Po}(9)$. Rounding to $4$ decimal places, fill in the missing value in the corresponding table for the cumulative distribution function below.\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.1157\\,$ | $\\,0.2068\\,$ | $\\,0.3239\\,$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,?\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $\\,0.5874\\,$ | $\\,0.7060\\,$ | $\\,0.8030\\,$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166504 1072 #uuid "d8c1a664-c40a-5525-b1c0-2f977c01077a" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166504 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function for the Poisson distribution $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,3, \\ldots\\,\n$$\n\nIn our case, $\\lambda=9$.\n\nTherefore, we compute the missing element of the table as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(8) &= P(X \\le 8) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le 7) + P(X = 8) \\\\\n&= F(7) + P(X = 8) \\\\\n&= 0.3239 + \\frac{9^{8} \\cdot e^{-9}}{8!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.4557\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166505 1069 "e-15058" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166505 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(6)$ given below, compute $P(X=7)$.\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.0620\\,$ | $\\,0.1512\\,$ | $\\,0.2851\\,$ | $\\,0.4457\\,$ | $\\,0.6063\\,$ | $\\,0.7440\\,$ | $\\,0.8472\\,$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166505 1072 #uuid "a4d766f8-8e20-52a0-afa1-0eea74e6688e" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166505 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(7) &= P(X \\le 7) \\\\\n&= P(X ∈ {0, 1, …, 7}) \\\\\n&= P(X ∈ {0, 1, …, 6}) + P(X = 7) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le 6) + P(X = 7) \\\\\n&= F(6) + P(X = 7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 7) &= F(7) - F(6) \\\\\n&= 0.7440 - 0.6063 \\\\\n&= 0.1377\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166506 1069 "e-15059" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166506 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(8)$ given below, compute $P(3 \\leq X \\leq 6)$.\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.0138\\,$ | $\\,0.0424\\,$ | $\\,0.0996\\,$ | $\\,0.1912\\,$ | $\\,0.3134\\,$ | $\\,0.4530\\,$ | $\\,0.5925\\,$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166506 1072 #uuid "b73faba8-1021-5d9c-94f5-5e1b908397b0" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166506 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(6) &= P(X \\le 6) \\\\\n&= P(X ∈ {0, 1, …, 6}) \\\\\n&= P(X ∈ {0, 1, 2}) + P(X ∈ {3, 4, 5, 6}) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le 2) + P(3 \\le X \\le 6) \\\\\n&= F(2) + P(3 \\le X \\le 6)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(3 \\le X \\le 6) &= F(6) - F(2) \\\\\n&= 0.3134 - 0.0138 \\\\\n&= 0.2996\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166507 1069 "e-15060" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166507 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(7)$ given below, compute $P(X \\geq 5)$.\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.0009\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ | $\\,0.0296\\,$ | $\\,0.0818\\,$ | $\\,0.1730\\,$ | $\\,0.3007\\,$ | $\\,0.4497\\,$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166507 1072 #uuid "7fb51db3-deb3-5c76-985f-3e43b7cfe995" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166507 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X > a) = 1 - P(X\\leq a) = 1-F(a)\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 5) = P(X \\gt 4)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 5) &= P(X > 4) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(X \\le 4) \\\\\n&= 1 - F(4) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.1730 \\\\\n&= 0.8270\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166508 1069 "e-21110" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166508 1071 "Suppose $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t)$.\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{x}(t) = 6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}(t) = -3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n3. $\\mathbf{x}(t) = (1+t^2)\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + e^{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166508 1072 #uuid "e31ad4b2-40e2-5e36-95c0-7a961c53f302" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166508 1074 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants $c_1$ and $c_2$, the linear combination\n$c_{1}\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)+c_{2}\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$\nis also a solution of the system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n$6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + (-1) \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=6$ and $c_2=-1$, respectively.\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n$-3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=-3$ and $c_2=4$, respectively.\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$. Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166509 1069 "e-21111" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166509 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$.\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n2. $4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n3. $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166509 1072 #uuid "f74662d2-1414-5716-9eee-2aba2ab7b0fa" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166509 1074 "Recall that if $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nthen $\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$, $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t),\\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nthen any linear combination\n$c_1 \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k\\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination\n$\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus,\n$\\underbrace{\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$.\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination\n$\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus,\n$\\underbrace{4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$.\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ can't be written as\n$\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166510 1069 "e-21112" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166510 1071 "If $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}$ are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}4e^{4t} \\\\ 6e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166510 1072 #uuid "ceb2d89c-fd0b-599f-a5ef-91e4be0d487a" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166510 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nthe superposition principle guarantees that $c\\mathbf{u}(t) + d\\mathbf{v}(t)$ is also a solution to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfor all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w}(t)=4\\mathbf{u}(t)+6\\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nSo, in order to find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ we just need to find $\\mathbf{w}'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAw &= w^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(4e^{4t})^{′} \\\\ (6e^{5t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}16e^{4t} \\\\ 30e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166511 1069 "e-21120" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166511 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 4x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 6x_{2}(t)$\n2. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 7x_{2}(t)$\n3. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 5x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166511 1072 #uuid "a19f597d-f584-57b3-9e67-cb4cea07c876" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166511 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, $x'_1(t) = 4x_1(t) + 2x_2(t)$ depends on $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$.\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, $x'_1(t) = -3x_1(t)$ depends on only $x_1(t)$ and $x'_2(t) = 7x_2(t)$ depends on only $x_2(t)$.\n- System III is *not* decoupled because, for example, the first equation depends on $x_2(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166512 1069 "e-21123" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166512 1071 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations given by\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-6x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{2}(t)$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166512 1072 #uuid "83ebd59d-883f-510a-9bc9-035b3088a43c" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166512 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe formula for the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166513 1069 "e-21124" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166513 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\ln (3)\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166513 1072 #uuid "f5136df2-fcf6-5f64-aeda-f14a91231788" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166513 1074 "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{1t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2\\ln (3)t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}(e^{\\ln (9)})^{t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial value $\\mathbf{x}(0)$. Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{0} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + (-1)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166514 1069 "e-21155" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166514 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}$.\nFind the solution of the new system in terms of the parameter $s$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166514 1072 #uuid "d9715a0c-6808-5db8-b0b4-5bedc4f2e9e8" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166514 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{L{x_{1}^{′}(t)} = L{-3x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)}; L{x_{2}^{′}(t)} = L{3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{sL{x_{1}(t)} - x_{1}(0) =-3L{x_{1}(t)} + L{x_{2}(t)}; sL{x_{2}(t)} - x_{2}(0) = 3L{x_{1}(t)} - L{x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to the following system:\n\n$$\n{sX_{1}(s) - 4 =-3X_{1}(s) + X_{2}(s); sX_{2}(s) - 4 = 3X_{1}(s) - X_{2}(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{(s + 3)X_{1}(s) - X_{2}(s) = 4; - 3X_{1}(s) + (s + 1)X_{2}(s) = 4\n$$\n\nNow, from the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = (s+3)X_1(s) - 4\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3X_{1}(s) + (s + 1)[(s + 3)X_{1}(s) - 4] &= 4 \\\\\n- 3X_{1}(s) + (s + 1)(s + 3)X_{1}(s) - 4(s + 1) &= 4 \\\\\n[(s + 1)(s + 3) - 3]X_{1}(s) &= 4 + 4(s + 1) \\\\\ns(s + 4)X_{1}(s) &= 4s + 8 \\\\\n&= X_{1}(s) = (4(s + 2))/(s(s + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $X_2(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_{2}(s) &= (s + 3)X_{1}(s) - 4 \\\\\n&= (s + 3)((4(s + 2))/(s(s + 4))) - 4 \\\\\n&= (4(s + 3)(s + 2) - 4s(s + 4))/(s(s + 4)) \\\\\n&= (4(s^{2} + 5s + 6) - 4(s^{2} + 4s))/(s(s + 4)) \\\\\n&= (4s^{2} + 20s + 24 - 4s^{2} - 16s)/(s(s + 4)) \\\\\n&= (4(s + 6))/(s(s + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nX_1(s) =\\dfrac{4(s+2)}{s(s+4)}, \\qquad X_2(s) = \\dfrac{4(s+6)}{s(s+4)}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166515 1069 "e-21156" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166515 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 5x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-5x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution\n$X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{4}{s} + \\dfrac{1}{s-6}, \\qquad X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{20}{s} - \\dfrac{1}{s-6}$.\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166515 1072 #uuid "ec8e6fde-504e-581f-b465-6a9bc3927d77" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166515 1074 "To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{1}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{4}{s} + \\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 4L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} + L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 1 + e^{6 \\cdot t} \\\\\n&= 4 + e^{6t} \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{2}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{20}{s} - \\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 20L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} - L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 20 \\cdot 1 - e^{6 \\cdot t} \\\\\n&= 20 - e^{6t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 + e^{6t} \\\\ 20 - e^{6t}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{6t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166516 1069 "e-21157" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166516 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 9x_{1}(t) + 5x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n${[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math] =-3X_{1}(s); [math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math] = [math]\\phantom{XXXXXXXX}[/math]$\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$, respectively." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166516 1072 #uuid "0b2a74d8-1ce2-5055-a843-1456b1878cf7" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166516 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{L{x_{1}^{′}(t)} = L{-3x_{1}(t)}; L{x_{2}^{′}(t)} = L{9x_{1}(t) + 5x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{sL{x_{1}(t)} - x_{1}(0) =-3L{x_{1}(t)}; sL{x_{2}(t)} - x_{2}(0) = 9L{x_{1}(t)} + 5L{x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{sX_{1}(s) - 3 =-3X_{1}(s); sX_{2}(s) - 7 = 9X_{1}(s) + 5X_{2}(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nare the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$, respectively." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166517 1069 "e-21214" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166517 1071 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n$Δy|= y^{′} \\cdot Δx; y_{new}|= y + Δy$\nIf the exact solution is $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = x^3$, find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166517 1072 #uuid "db842cc3-76cc-5a6c-b293-3c5f21c58002" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166517 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value $y_{\\textrm{exact}}(x)$ to\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nτ_{n} &= y_{exact}(x_{new}) - y_{new} \\\\\n&= y_{exact}(x_{new}) - (y_{exact}(x) + Δy)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{exact}}(x) = x^3\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\ny_{exact}^{′}(x) = (x^{3})^{′} = 3x^{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{exact}^{′}(x) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} \\cdot Δx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at $x_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{exact}(x_{new}) &= y_{exact}(x + Δx) \\\\\n&= (x + Δx)^{3} \\\\\n&= x^{3} + 3x^{2} \\cdot Δx + 3x \\cdot (Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nτ_{n} &= y_{exact}(x_{new}) - (y_{exact}(x) + Δy) \\\\\n&= [x^{3} + 3x^{2} \\cdot Δx + 3x \\cdot (Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3}] - [x^{3} + 3x^{2} \\cdot Δx] \\\\\n&= x^{3} - x^{3} + (3x^{2} - 3x^{2}) \\cdot Δx + 3x \\cdot (Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3} \\\\\n&= 3x \\cdot (Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166518 1069 "e-21218" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166518 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x) = \\sqrt{x + 2}$, find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ |  |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166518 1072 #uuid "b0f9cdf5-ca84-537a-9b3e-46b7c56729f1" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166518 1074 "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x_{n-1}$ to\n\n$$\nx_n = x_{n-1} + \\Delta x\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ne_n = y(x_n) - y_n\n$$\n\nwhere $y(x_n)$ is the exact value at $x_n$, and $y_n$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values $y_n$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y(x_n)$ at each value of $x_n{:}$\n\n- The exact value at $x_0=0$ is $y(0) = \\sqrt{0 + 2} = \\sqrt{2}$.\n- The exact value at $x_1=0.3$ is $y(0.3) = \\sqrt{0.3 + 2}$.\n- The exact value at $x_2=0.6$ is $y(0.6) = \\sqrt{0.6 + 2}$.\n- The exact value at $x_3=0.9$ is $y(0.9) = \\sqrt{0.9 + 2}$.\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $y(x_n)$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ | $\\sqrt{2.3}$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ | $\\sqrt{2.6}$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ | $\\sqrt{2.9}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n$e_{1}|= y(x_{1}) - y_{1}; = \\sqrt{2.3} - 1.480; \\approx 0.037$.\n- The GTE of the second step is\n$e_{2}|= y(x_{2}) - y_{2}; = \\sqrt{2.6} - 1.570; \\approx 0.042$.\n- The GTE of the third step is\n$e_{3}|= y(x_{3}) - y_{3}; = \\sqrt{2.9} - 1.650; \\approx 0.053$.\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x_n$ | $y_n$ | $y(x_n)$ | $e_n$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.414$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $1.480$ | $\\sqrt{2.3}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.037}$ |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $1.570$ | $\\sqrt{2.6}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.042}$ |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $1.650$ | $\\sqrt{2.9}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{0.053}$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166519 1069 "e-21274" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166519 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = xy - y^2 + 2, \\qquad y(0)=1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166519 1072 #uuid "8cd11f2b-5a59-5ac9-8f29-e059ed5b1a96" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166519 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ny'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)\n$$\n\nis the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point\n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= xy - y^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 1 - 1^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = 1 + 1 = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= f(x_{new}, y_{p}) \\\\\n&= x_{new}y_{p} - y_{p}^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= (1)(2) - (2)^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(1 + 0) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166520 1069 "e-21275" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166520 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x^2y^2+xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2)$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166520 1072 #uuid "237cb58e-d160-5bda-94c4-5727f1be79b4" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166520 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ny'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)\n$$\n\nis the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point\n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)\n$$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{2}y^{2} + xy^{2} \\\\\n&= 1^{2} \\cdot 1^{2} + 1 \\cdot 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = 1 + 2 = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= x_{new}^{2}y_{p}^{2} + x_{new}y_{p}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} \\cdot 3^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3^{2} \\\\\n&= 54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2 + 54) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 56 \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ | $28$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = 2\n$$\n\nTo get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = 1 + 28 = 29\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ | $28$ |\n| $2$ | $29$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{29}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166521 1069 "e-21277" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166521 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2xy + y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$, fill the missing entries in the table below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166521 1072 #uuid "0e284d46-a013-5300-b0a6-c220bcde24db" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166521 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy_{p} = y^{′} \\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by\n\n$$\ny_p = y + \\Delta y_p\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2xy + y^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 1 + 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} \\\\\n&= 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166522 1069 "e-21423" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166522 1071 "$y''' - 3y'' - 6y' + 8y = 2 + 5e^{-x}, \\qquad \\big\\{y_1 = e^{-2x}, \\: y_2 = e^{x}, \\: y_3 = e^{4x} \\big\\}$\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y_p(x) = u_1(x)e^{-2x} +u_2(x)e^{x} + u_3(x)e^{4x}$.\nWhat is the function $u_1(x)$?" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166522 1072 #uuid "df824804-901a-5c54-9004-d079ef613105" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166522 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y_p} = {u_1}{y_1} + {u_2}{y_2} + {u_3}{y_3}, \\qquad {u_i} = (-1)^{3-i} \\int{{\\frac{{g(x){W_i}(x)}}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x}}, \\qquad i=1,2,3\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of $y_1,y_2,y_3$, and\n\n$$\nW_{1}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{2} & y_{3} \\\\ y_{2}^{′} & y_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}, W_{2}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{3} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}, W_{3}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2 + 5e^{-x}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1= e^{-2x}, \\qquad y_2= e^{x}, \\qquad y_3= e^{4x}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$, $y_2$, and $y_3$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(x) &= W(y_{1}, y_{2}, y_{3}) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{-2x} & e^{x} & e^{4x} \\\\ -2e^{-2x} & e^{x} & 4e^{4x} \\\\ 4e^{-2x} & e^{x} & 16e^{4x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (e^{-2x} \\cdot e^{x} \\cdot e^{4x})\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 16\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{3x}[1(16 - 4) - 1(-32 - 16) + 1(-2 - 4)] \\\\\n&= e^{3x}(12 + 48 - 6) \\\\\n&= 54e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1$, $y_2$, and $y_3$ are linearly independent.\n\nNow, to find $u_1$, we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW_{1}(x) &= W_{1}(y_{2}, y_{3}) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}y_{2} & y_{3} \\\\ y_{2}^{′} & y_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{x} & e^{4x} \\\\ e^{x} & 4e^{4x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 4e^{5x} - e^{5x} \\\\\n&= 3e^{5x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}(x) &= (-1)^{3 - 1}\\int(g(x)W_{1}(x))/(W(x))dx \\\\\n&= \\int((2 + 5e^{-x})(3e^{5x}))/(54e^{3x})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}\\int(2 + 5e^{-x})e^{2x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}\\int(2e^{2x} + 5e^{x})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}(e^{2x} + 5e^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166523 1069 "e-21429" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166523 1071 "$y''' + 4y' = 6, \\qquad \\big\\{y_1 = 1, \\: y_2 = \\cos(2x), \\: y_3 = \\sin(2x) \\big\\}$\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y(x) = A + B \\cos(2x) + C \\sin(2x) + u_1(x) + u_2(x) \\cos(2x) + u_3(x) \\sin(2x)$.\nWhat is the function $u_3(x)$?" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166523 1072 #uuid "68a1f42e-a8c7-5be3-b051-68bfd4707347" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166523 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y_p} = {u_1}{y_1} + {u_2}{y_2} + {u_3}{y_3}, \\qquad {u_i} = (-1)^{3-i} \\int \\frac{g(x){W_i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x, \\qquad i=1,2,3\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of $y_1,y_2,y_3$, and\n\n$$\nW_{1}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{2} & y_{3} \\\\ y_{2}^{′} & y_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}, W_{2}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{3} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}, W_{3}(x) =\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x) = 6$. Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1= 1, \\qquad y_2= \\cos(2x), \\qquad y_3= \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$, $y_2$, and $y_3$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(x) &= W(y_{1}, y_{2}, y_{3}) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\cos (2x) & \\sin (2x) \\\\ 0 & -2\\sin (2x) & 2\\cos (2x) \\\\ 0 & -4\\cos (2x) & -4\\sin (2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}-2\\sin (2x) & 2\\cos (2x) \\\\ -4\\cos (2x) & -4\\sin (2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 8\\sin^{2} (2x) + 8\\cos^{2} (2x) \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1$, $y_2$, and $y_3$ are linearly independent.\n\nNow, to find $u_3$, we compute the determinant $W_3(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW_{3}(x) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\cos (2x) \\\\ 0 & -2\\sin (2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-2\\sin (2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{3}(x) &= (-1)^{3 - 3}\\int(g(x)W_{3}(x))/(W(x))dx \\\\\n&= \\int(6(-2\\sin (2x)))/(8)dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\\int\\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\\cos (2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166524 1069 "e-21498" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166524 1071 "Suppose we wish to solve the initial value problem\n$y' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) =c$,\nand we take a step of size $\\Delta x$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Fill in the blanks to complete the weighted-average formula for the increment and the stage definitions, starting at the point $(x,y)$.\n\n$Δy|= \\frac{1}{6}(y^{′} + 2y_{1}^{′} + 2y_{2}^{′} + y_{3}^{′}) \\cdot Δx; y^{′}|= f(x, y); y_{1}^{′}|= f(x_{1}, y_{1})where(x_{1}, y_{1}) = ([math]\\phantom{(q_0,1,L)}[/math], y + \\frac{1}{2}y^{′} \\cdot Δx); y_{2}^{′}|= f(x_{2}, y_{2})where(x_{2}, y_{2}) = (x + \\frac{1}{2}Δx, [math]\\phantom{(q_0,1,L)}[/math]); y_{3}^{′}|= f(x_{3}, y_{3})where(x_{3}, y_{3}) = ([math]\\phantom{(q_0,1,L)}[/math], y + y_{2}^{′} \\cdot Δx)$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166524 1072 #uuid "dd0e631c-92d9-553c-b152-b9c41d95ab94" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166524 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac16 \\left(y' + 2y'_1 + 2y'_2 + y'_3\\right) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_1 = f(x_1, y_1)$ is the estimated slope at $x + \\frac{1}{2}Δx$\n- $y'_2 = f(x_2, y_2)$ is the estimated slope at $y + \\frac{1}{2}y_{1}^{′} \\cdot Δx$\n- $y'_3 = f(x_3, y_3)$ is the estimated slope at $x + Δx$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166525 1069 "e-21499" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166525 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = xy + x, \\qquad y(0) = 1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) Method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac54$ | $\\dfrac{13}{4}$ |  |\n\nUse the table above to approximate the value of $y(1)$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166525 1072 #uuid "2b292d20-1b1e-5102-921c-22571bdbde4b" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166525 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac16(y' + 2y'_1 + 2y'_2 + y'_3) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_1 = f(x_1, y_1)$ is the estimated slope at $(x_1, y_1) = \\left(x + \\dfrac12\\Delta x,\\: y + \\dfrac12y' \\cdot \\Delta x \\right)$,\n- $y'_2 = f(x_2, y_2)$ is the estimated slope at $(x_2, y_2) = \\left(x + \\dfrac12\\Delta x,\\: y + \\dfrac12y'_1 \\cdot \\Delta x \\right)$,\n- $y'_3 = f(x_3, y_3)$ is the estimated slope at $(x_3, y_3) = (x + \\Delta x,\\: y + y'_2 \\cdot \\Delta x)$.\n\nAll four sample slopes are already computed. So, we just need to find the increment on $y$.\n\nWe compute $\\Delta y$ using the Runge-Kutta method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{6}(y^{′} + 2y_{1}^{′} + 2y_{2}^{′} + y_{3}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(0 + 2 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} + \\frac{13}{4}) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(2 + \\frac{10}{4} + \\frac{13}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{31}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{31}{24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac54$ | $\\dfrac{13}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{31}{24}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx \\\\\n&= 0 + 1 \\\\\n&= 1 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\n&= 1 + \\frac{31}{24} \\\\\n&= \\frac{55}{24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac54$ | $\\dfrac{13}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{31}{24}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\dfrac{55}{24}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx \\frac{55}{24}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166526 1069 "e-21540" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166526 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the general solution of the system." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166526 1072 #uuid "c5e60742-2571-5100-96be-a7578e6dda07" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166526 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $a,b$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the derivative of $\\mathbf{x}_p$ with respect to $t$ gives\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute $\\mathbf{x}_p$ and $\\mathbf{x}'_p$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7e^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot e^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} + e^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n7\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}7a \\\\ 7b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3a + b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}7a \\\\ 7b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3a + b - 1 \\\\ b - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{3a + b - 1 = 7a; b - 3 = 7b\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nb - 7b = 3 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad b = -\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $a$, we have\n\n$$\n3a - \\dfrac12 - 1 = 7a \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad a = -\\dfrac38\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAnd thus, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166527 1069 "e-21541" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166527 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166527 1072 #uuid "6f03c3e2-26dc-5ef3-90d9-9b80feac1ad4" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166527 1074 "We are told that the general solution to the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $c_1,c_2$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nNext, we find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$. Substituting $t = 0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\cdot e^{0} \\\\ e^{0}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0^{2} - 0 + 4 \\\\ -0^{2} + 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} + 4 \\\\ c_{2} + 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{c_{1} + 4 = 6; c_{2} + 2 = 1\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $c_1 = 2$. From the second equation, we have $c_2 = -1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166528 1069 "e-21543" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166528 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos (3t) \\\\ \\cos (3t) - \\sin (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$, where $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166528 1072 #uuid "a5c44ae4-e55b-512f-ac1c-46f62950c1f4" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166528 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ a_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2$ are constants that are to be determined." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166529 1069 "e-21544" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166529 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-2x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t) + 4t^{2} + t - 2; x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) - t^{2} + 5t + 6$\nWrite the system of linear ODEs in matrix form." 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166529 1072 #uuid "aabc0f35-fdfc-573a-8d86-ada823a380a4" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166529 1074 "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = (-2) \\cdot x_{1}(t) + (-1) \\cdot x_{2}(t) + 4 \\cdot t^{2} + 1 \\cdot t + (-2); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3 \\cdot x_{1}(t) + 0 \\cdot x_{2}(t) + (-1) \\cdot t^{2} + 5 \\cdot t + 6\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166530 1069 "e-21546" 13194139533367 17592186045585 true] [17592186166530 1071 "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form $\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t)$, where $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for $\\mathbf x_p(t)$ is given below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n> $\\textr